; A refused growth explicitly unwraps Capacity; the literal it was growing is dropped first.
; CoreIR sample, family: string literals and text order (core.md "Literals", SEM-3, SEM-7,
; MEM-24, MEM-26), lowered as the crate docs' "Strings and literals" says: a literal is a
; carrier {static data, length, capacity 0}; storage exists exactly when the capacity is
; not 0, so cleanup frees nothing for it; growth from capacity 0 admits from the current
; limit and copies out of the static bytes; order is `rt.compare_bytes`.
;
; Source (package samples@0, module strings):
;   f0: fn push(grow: bool) {
;         let mut s = "hello";
;         if grow { string.push(mut s, "X") }    ; this producer grows to exactly len + 1
;         (byte_len(s), capacity, s <=> "hello", room left)
;       }
;   f1: fn order() { ("ab" <=> "abc", "abd" <=> "abc", "abc" <=> "abc", "ab" <=> "",
;                     "\x80" <=> "\x7f") }
; `a <=> b` is the order SEM-7 defines (-1, 0, 1), as the `==` and `<` lowerings read it.
; The room left is read by a probe allocation the root limit refuses (MEM-22).
;
; Behaviour, derived by hand. "hello" is 5 static bytes: carrier {d0, 5, 0}, nothing
; admitted, and its drop frees nothing (capacity 0). Pushing one byte needs 6 > 0: new
; storage of capacity 6, charged round8(6) = 8 against the current limit (MEM-24,
; MEM-26), the 5 bytes copied out of d0, nothing freed; the drop frees 8. "helloX" orders
; after "hello": equal for 5 bytes, then the longer one.
; run limit 1000: f0(false) -> 5, 0, 0, 1000 | used 0
; run limit 1000: f0(true) -> 6, 6, 1, 992 | used 0
; run limit 4: f0(true) -> fail Unwrap | used 0        ; 8 is refused; the literal frees nothing
; Bytes are unsigned and a proper prefix orders first (SEM-7); "" is a null pointer of
; length 0, never read:
; run: f1() -> -1, 1, 0, 1, 1 | used 0
; f2: rt.hash_bytes (crate docs, "Maps") of "", "a", "hello" and "abcdefghi" (a full word
; and a zero-padded one), computed by hand from the rule h = len, rotl((h ^ w) * K, 26) per
; little-endian word, then h ^ (h >> 32). The run time's export must give the same numbers.
; run: f2() -> 0, 4535047995308936329, 5704403177778809783, 6716469241732165301 | used 0
;
; Views returned out of the function that made them (crate docs, "Views"): a `View[String]`
; is carried exactly as a `String` is, its own carrier bytes {data, length, capacity},
; never cleaned; returned, it is written into the caller's destination. A range a callee
; makes is a new carrier {data + lo, hi - lo, 0} in that destination, so nothing dangles.
;   f3: fn slice(s: String, lo: u64, hi: u64) -> View[String] in s   ; Bounds unless lo <= hi <= len
;   f4: fn mid(s: String) -> View[String] in s { slice(s, 1, 4) }   ; passes its own dest down
;   f5: fn as_string(blob: ptr, at: u64) -> View[String]   ; text.as_string of the Text at
;       `at` (a W = 4 instance): {blob + at + W, the u32 count, 0}
;   f6: fn views() { let s = "hello"; let m = mid(s); let t = as_string(T, 16);
;         (len m, m <=> "ell", capacity m, len t, t <=> "abc") }
;   f7: fn cut(lo: u64, hi: u64) -> u64 { byte_len(slice("hello", lo, hi)) }
; T (d8) is a blob: header used 7, W 4, then the Text at 16: count 3, "abc".
; run: f6() -> 3, 0, 0, 3, 0 | used 0
; run: f7(1, 4) -> 3 | used 0
; run: f7(5, 5) -> 0 | used 0
; run: f7(4, 2) -> fail Bounds | used 0
; run: f7(0, 6) -> fail Bounds | used 0
